                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Great game idea heavily impacted by very poor (IMO) execution and programming.\n\nFirst the good; I love the characters, and the story premise, while completely trope, is entertaining.\n\nNow for the load of bad;\n\nThe game itself runs on the Steam Deck, but tries its best to fight it. Won't recognize the controller parts, so you have  to either play it solely as a touchpad mouse game, or design your own controller layout.\nSaving the game automatically brings up the on-screen keyboard, and while exiting out of it will quite often soft-lock the game.\nNo cloud saves. So can't play the same save on PC and Steam Deck.\n\nPC gameplay is what it was designed for, and runs well on practically any PC not in the scrapyard yet. But...\n\nGame is very short. Even though you can go up to lvl 40, many finish at around lvl 10 in four to five hours. I like to have a price versus gameplay ratio of $1 per hour of gameplay. Even at $5 the game is marginal when it  comes to \"bang for the buck\".\nEvery level up you only get one single point to distribute in eight or more attributes, making it ludicrously meaningless, except for the fact that...\nThe higher any special ability, the more costly it is to cast, with greatly diminished, even detrimental returns. You can cast a level one ability ten times at full power, or five times with a half damage increase only five times, level three at double damage but only three times, ect. So basackwards it's truly jaw-droppingly idio...err...detrimental.\nMaze puzzle events that serve absolutely no other purpose than to pad out game completion time. Thankfully can be skipped with the H key with no detriment to achievement hunters.\n\nBasically, IMO, as much as I like the characters and story premise, all enjoyment ends there, so there's no way I'd ever, in good conscience, be able to recommend this game to friends or family, so I can't recommend it to anyone else either.",
